\label{thm:path}
Let $F \subseteq \mathbf{R}$ be a countable real-closed subfield. There exists a unital $F$-subalgebra $A\subseteq \mathbf{R}$ of countable index (as subgroup) and a one-parameter family of unital ring homomorphisms
\begin{equation*}
\varphi_\tau\colon A\longrightarrow\mathbf{R} \kern3mm (0\leq \tau \leq1)
\end{equation*}
each fixing $F$ pointwise and such that:
\begin{enumerate}[label=\normalfont(\roman*)]
 \item $\varphi_0(A)=F$;
 \item $\varphi_1$ is the inclusion $A\hookrightarrow\mathbf{R}$;
 \item for every $x\in A$, the map $\tau\mapsto\varphi_\tau(x)$ is continuous on $[0,1]$.
\end{enumerate}

Let $\xi\in U$ be the vanishing point in the statement and write $z=(z_1, \ldots, z_q)$. By the real-analytic identity theorem, it suffices to prove that the germ of $f$ at $\xi$ is zero. If not, then its minimal polynomial over $F(z)$
\begin{equation*}
f^d+r_{d-1}(z)f^{d-1}+\cdots+r_0(z)=0
\end{equation*}
has nonzero constant coefficient $r_0$. Algebraic independence implies that every nonzero polynomial over $F$ is nonzero at $\xi$. Thus all the rational functions $r_j$ are defined there and $r_0(\xi)\ne0$. Evaluating the equation contradicts $f(\xi)=0$.

\label{lem:tail}
For every $x\in \mathbf{R}$ we have:
\begin{enumerate}[label=\normalfont(\roman*)]
\item $f_{x,n}(1)=f_{x,n+1}(0)$ for all sufficiently large $n$,\label{lem:tail:coincide}

\item $\lim\limits_{n\to\infty}\,\sup\limits_{0\leq t\leq1}|f_{x,n}(t)-x| = 0$.\qed \label{lem:tail:limit}
\end{enumerate}

\label{ref:ext}
The proof of the special case of Theorem~\ref{thm:new} can be phrased elementarily as follows. Consider the subgroup $\Gamma <\operatorname{Ad}(G)$ defined above; we need to show that it can be lifted to $G$. The adjoint representation is, topologically, a covering map $G \to \operatorname{Ad}(G)$. Therefore, given any $\gamma\in \Gamma$, the homotopy lifting property and $f_0(\Gamma)=\{e\}$ implies that there is a unique lift of paths
\begin{equation*}
\tau \longmapsto \widetilde f_\tau(\gamma) \in G \kern3mm \text{such that}\kern3mm \operatorname{Ad}\big(\widetilde f_\tau(\gamma) \big) = f_\tau(\gamma)  \kern3mm \text{and}\kern3mm \widetilde f_0(\gamma) =e.
\end{equation*}
By uniqueness, it follows that $\widetilde f_\tau$ is a group homomorphism for any given $\tau$. Now $\widetilde f_1(\Gamma)$ is the desired group lift of $\Gamma$.
